them to go back they want to go back  so i don't think people generally want  to move further afield right now they  want to be able to go back to their  homes and their lives as as soon as  they're able to do so all right i just  want to step back for a moment and look  at another angle of all of this african  students trying to leave ukraine are  denouncing ukrainian and polish  authorities on social media videos  posted on twitter show large crowds of  young people of african descent stuck at  the border since the invasion began take  a look  we are not allowing any black people to  enter inside the gates  we are all here it's only ukrainians  that they're allowing in  even the walls with kids they're not  allowing them  here  for days and nobody's asking any  questions  only ukrainian woman and  he's they're allowing into this gate  all the places blocked and all are  pulled up blacks  from different communities  from different countries nobody's  talking to us  poland's response now is very different  to last year when soldiers stopped  thousands of mainly syrian afghan and  kurdish refugees crossing over from  belarus it's also in contrast to how  european nations dealt with more than  one million refugees who entered during  the 2015 migrant crisis  olay let me ask you about this um  i'm struck by the fact that the response  is so different right now i spent a lot  of time in europe in 2015 covering the  refugee crisis and one of the borders  that i spent a lot of time was the  serbian border with hungary  and i saw refugees mainly from  syria  iraq afghanistan other countries  that were forced back into serbia by  patrol men on horses  a lot of these refugees men women and  children  were beaten back they were injured when  trying to come in  to hungary  and of course there's been a lot of  controversy these past several years  about the eu's migration policy  when you see how different the response  is right now to refugees from ukraine  who obviously should be given safe  harbor but when you see how different  the response is to the refugees from  ukraine  to  what the actions were when it was  refugees coming from  syria iraq afghanistan and other  countries what do you make of that  well i think this is something that we  need to  reflect over and i think you know this  is a  it's an incredibly important principle  in international law that anybody who is  in need of safety should receive it  where they where they might be able to  get it and this has been the position of  the norwegian refugee council as well in  the last in these last several years as  we have seen people from other places  other uh around the world seeking refuge  in in europe is that we have to uh let  those with real protection need in and  to make sure that they are safe as well  um you know it is uh it is as you say uh  a um  and sort of a very conspicuous  difference here in terms of how uh how  european countries have reacted to these  different situations uh but what i hope  perhaps is that we can take with us kind  of some of the lessons or reflections  from this crisis in in ukraine and how  we have responded to that so that we  also can have more empathy from  to uh for people who are coming from  other parts of the world and that we see  i hope that we will see a more open  attitude to those people as well going  forward  jung ah the german interior minister has  said that europe should agree on  accepting ukrainian refugees without  bureaucratic hurdles  from your perspective from your point of  view do you think that this crisis may  change the way that the eu deals with  migration going forward  it's very possible i mean obviously it's  early days and and things are just  changing not only daily but hourly but i  do think that we do have precedence in  in other situations during the bosnian  conflict for example during the kosovo  conflict we did have uh situations where  um with the the conflict in europe um  different european countries did come to  agreements on on how  many refugees they would accept and also  what uh what would be the the rights and  the services that they would be allowed  so already i i think that these  discussions are taking place um we're  glad that they're taking place sooner  rather than later  but i think right now it's it's uh  humanitarian access and humanitarian aid  that is the most important need on the  ground  maria when you speak to  friends of yours colleagues in ukraine  who have made that extremely difficult  and traumatic decision to leave  what are they telling you are are they  telling you that they believe they will  only be gone a short time or do they  fear that this could last a very long  time and that they could be away from  home  for  much longer than they had anticipated  well most of my friends and colleagues  who have to leave their homes because  from  some of them did want to stay  so they went to the to to places inside  ukraine to the west of ukraine to the  central ukraine that is still  not uh safe there because  russia shells also that areas as well  but still it is of course safer than for  example in kharkiv now or in kiev and  what they want is they want to come back  as soon as possible to their homes to be  to return to their normal lives because  we are a big nation of 40 million people  we don't want you know to run we want to  fight and we want to  have control over ukrainian territory uh  which is uh according to the  international laws so uh i have myself  been offered many times by my friends a  place where i could stay in poland in  germany in macedonia in in moldova in  other countries because now everyone  here is  giving hand to ukraine and european  nations are united in in this so uh i  was offered that help and i'm really  thankful to to all of  those who  who help ukraine now but i as i have  said i do not want to leave  because i don't have any reasons for  that it's russian soldiers who have to  leave our territory and speaking of  those few who have  actually left and went to  poland for example or moldova  they usually make this because  they are really afraid and they have  small children and they are threatened  that if they will stay in ukraine even  in western ukraine where there are also  air strikes now that the children  can be harmed and  so they're protecting their small ones  olay obviously there is great concern  for the people of ukraine for those who  are staying for those who have decided  to leave but i want to ask you about  another aspect of this is there concern  on the part of you and your colleagues  for the safety of the humanitarian  workers that are responding to all of  this  yeah of course there is i mean we now  have you know uh our teams from the  norwegian refugee council they're in  kiev they're in  sierras which is a city that is fairly  close to what used to be the front lines  between government and  non-government-controlled areas and they  are hunkering down in in bomb shelters  uh as everybody else um and and as we  talked about i think it is incredibly  important that that they are able to  then when things stabilize a little bit  uh work uh work freely but i think you  know the most important part now for me  is really to keep a focus on the  civilians who are caught in this this  sort of unnecessary war and in areas of  active fighting we saw back from 2014  and 2015  that both sides ended up using weapons  that were  extremely dangerous to civilians area  weapons and we've seen confirmation of  such use of weapons again now  so i'm really worried that we will see  both displacement but we'll also see  much more damage to infrastructure to  houses  killings and injuries of civilians  and this is really what our focus should  be on now to try to stop that there  should be maximum diplomatic pressure to  stop that to not use such weapons and  then we need to scale up the  humanitarian assistance  so that we can address the needs that  are are going to be absolutely  staggering  it is a terrifying situation um very  concerning for so many of the people  inside of ukraine right now  we have run out of time so we're going  to have to leave the discussion there  thank you so much to all of our guests  maria avdiva jung gadini williams and  ole solvang  and thank you two for watching you can  see the program again anytime by  visiting our website aljazeera.com and  for further discussion go to our  facebook page that's facebook.com  forward slash aj inside story you can  also join the conversation on twitter  our handle is at aj inside story from me  muhammad jim jim the whole team here bye  for now  [Music]  you
 

